WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.071
Assumptions are beliefs or conditions that reasoning depends on.

00:00:03.071 --> 00:00:07.032
Some are explicit, such as assuming a budget limit or a deadline.

00:00:07.032 --> 00:00:10.993
Others are hidden, such as assuming users will behave a certain way,

00:00:10.993 --> 00:00:12.644
a data source is complete,

00:00:12.644 --> 00:00:15.945
or a team will adopt a new process without resistance.

00:00:15.945 --> 00:00:20.566
Critical thinking asks what are we assuming before the group commits to a conclusion.

00:00:20.566 --> 00:00:24.528
Problem framing determines what information is noticed and what solutions seem reasonable.

00:00:24.528 --> 00:00:28.159
If a system outage is framed only as an engineering mistake,

00:00:28.159 --> 00:00:30.469
the team may focus on individual performance.

00:00:30.469 --> 00:00:35.091
If it is framed as a resilience problem, the team may examine architecture, monitoring,

00:00:35.091 --> 00:00:36.411
handoff, runbooks, and workload.

00:00:36.411 --> 00:00:40.703
The frame does not decide the answer, but it shapes where attention goes.

00:00:40.703 --> 00:00:43.013
Scope and definitions are part of framing.

00:00:43.013 --> 00:00:47.305
A team discussing secure design needs to agree whether it means code quality,

00:00:47.305 --> 00:00:50.276
identity controls, threat modeling, supply chain risk, deployment configuration,

00:00:50.276 --> 00:00:51.596
or all of those.

00:00:51.596 --> 00:00:55.227
Without shared definitions, people may appear aligned while defending different conclusions.

00:00:55.227 --> 00:00:58.528
Clear definitions reduce false agreement and make disagreement more useful.

00:00:58.528 --> 00:01:00.839
Context can change the meaning of evidence.

00:01:00.839 --> 00:01:05.130
A process that works in a small team may fail at enterprise scale.

00:01:05.130 --> 00:01:10.082
A control that is reasonable in a low-risk environment may be inadequate for regulated data.

00:01:10.082 --> 00:01:11.402
Incentives, stakeholders, constraints, history,

00:01:11.402 --> 00:01:14.703
and timing all affect what counts as a practical answer.

00:01:14.703 --> 00:01:19.325
Critical thinkers look at the environment around the claim, not only the claim itself.

00:01:19.325 --> 00:01:21.305
Before committing, ask several framing questions.

00:01:21.305 --> 00:01:23.946
What problem are we actually trying to solve?

00:01:23.946 --> 00:01:25.927
What is inside and outside scope?

00:01:25.927 --> 00:01:26.917
Who is affected?

00:01:26.917 --> 00:01:28.567
What incentives are at work?

00:01:28.567 --> 00:01:30.218
What would change our interpretation?

00:01:30.218 --> 00:01:34.179
These questions help prevent a team from solving a simplified version of

00:01:34.179 --> 00:01:36.820
the problem while the real conditions remain untouched.
